course: dictionary
lesson: 10348
title: Translation: words, shapes and new forms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A translation can mean changing words from one language into another. «Translation tools help families who speak other languages understand letters, but they can make mistakes.» Here a translation is the new version of the letter, written in a language the family can read.
explain: Translation has a different meaning in maths. A translation slides a shape to a new place without turning it, flipping it or changing its size. You can describe it with directions, like 3 right, 2 up.
explain: Some phrases use this word too. «Lost in translation» means some of the meaning went missing when words were changed into another language. «Puns hardly ever survive translation», because a joke that depends on two meanings of one word often stops being funny in another language.
fact: Translation is a noun. A translation is one version of a text, written or spoken in another language.
fact: «Important translations should be checked by a person who speaks both languages.»
fact: In a maths translation, every point of a shape moves the same distance in the same direction, so the shape stays the same size and shape.
fact: Phrases to know: «in translation», «a translation into», «lost in translation» and «translation of a shape».
fact: In science, translation means changing something into a different form.
exercise: «Important translations should be checked by a person who speaks both languages.» What is a translation in this sentence? | A shape moved 3 right and 2 up | A sound made by a computer | A list of fifty kitten names | *A piece of writing changed into another language
exercise: A triangle is slid 3 right and 2 up on a grid, but it is not turned or flipped. What is this? | A change of colour | *A translation in maths | A reflection in a mirror | A change into another language
exercise: «Puns hardly ever survive translation.» What does the phrase «lost in translation» suggest? | The book was lost in the post | *Some of the meaning goes missing when words change language | The translator forgot every word | The translation is longer than the original
test: In a translation of a shape, does the shape change its size? | Yes, it gets smaller | It depends on the grid | *No, it stays the same size | Yes, it gets bigger
test: Which of these is a translation in a language lesson? | Flipping a photo over | Drawing a circle with a compass | *Turning «I love you, Granny» into Polish | Sliding a chess piece across the board
exam: Which sentence uses translation correctly? | The shape was translation and turned upside down | *A translation of the shape stays the same size, moving 2 right and 1 up | Translation tools can help families understand a letter in another language | Translation is a colour we use in art
